# {{lb|en|uncountable}} [[recognition|Recognition]] of one or more [[past#Adjective|past]] [[mistake#Noun|mistakes]], especially with a [[desire#Noun|desire]] to [[improve]] in the [[future#Noun|future]]; [[repentance]]; {{lb|en|countable}} an [[instance#Noun|instance]] of this. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1570|author=&#91;[[w:Alexander Nowell|Alexander Nowell]]&#93;|chapter=The Second Part. Of the Gospell and Faith.|translator=T[homas] Norton|title=A Catechisme, or First Instruction and Learning of Christian Religion.{{nb...|Translated out of Latine into Englishe.}}|location=London|publisher={{...|Printed by}} [[w:John Day (printer)|Iohn Daye]]{{nb...|dwelling ouer Aldersgate.}}|section=folios 47, recto&nbsp;– 48, verso|sectionurl=https://archive.org/details/bim_early-english-books-1475-1640_a-catechisme-or-first-i_nowell-alexander_1570/page/n102/mode/1up|oclc=1203214376|passage=And I haue already ſayd how ſinners for obteyning of pardon haue neede of repentance, which ſome like better to call '''Reſipiſcence''', or amendment, and of change of minde: and the [[w:Jesus|Lord]] promiſeth that he will pardon ſinners if they repent, if they amend and turne their hartes from their naughtie liues vnto hym.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=['''1622''']|author=[[w:Peter Canisius|P[eter] Canisius]]|chapter=Of Indulgences, or Pardons|translator=&#91;{{w|Henry Garnet}}&#93;|title=A Summe of Christian Doctrine:{{nb...|Composed in Latin, by the R. Father P. Canisius, of the Society of Iesus. With an Appendix of the Fall of Man & Iustification, according to the Doctrine of the Councel of Trent. Newly Translated into Englishe. To which is Adioined the Explication of Certaine Questions Not Handled at Large in the Booke as shall Appeare in the Table.}}|location=Saint-Omer|publisher={{...|Printed [by]}} &#91;[[w:fr:Charles Boscart|Charles Boscart]]&#93; for [[w:John Heigham|Iohn Heigham]],{{nb...|with permission of superiors, At S. Omers}}|page=644|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=fzsSDLTh008C&pg=PA644|oclc=1351263203|passage=[T]hey vvill not haue to ſignifie a ſorrovvefull repentance, but a '''{{smallcaps|Resipiscence}}''', or vvaxing vviſe againe vvhich God graunt they may doe vvithout any ſorrovve, or affliction of minde for that vvhich is paſt.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year='''1796''' (date written)|author=[[w:John Maclaurin, Lord Dreghorn|John Maclaurin]]|chapter=Address to the Powers at War|title=The Works of the Late John Maclaurin, Esq. of Dreghorn:{{nb...|One of the Senators of the College of Justice, and F.R.S. Edin&#691;. [...] In Two Volumes.}}|location=Edinburgh|publisher={{...|Printed for the editor, by}} J. Ruthven and Sons; and sold by Bell & Bradfute, and E. Balfour|year_published=1798|volume=I|page=177|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=xuMwAAAAIAAJ&pg=PA177|oclc=1372000935|passage=[R]ecollect your conduct paſt, / That '''reſipiſc'''{{quote-gloss|'''e'''}}'''nce''' may at laſt / Effectuate a change.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year='''1817''' [May?] (date written)|author=w:Thomas Jefferson|chapter=To {{w|George Ticknor}} [letter]|editor=w:Paul Leicester Ford|title=The Works of Thomas Jefferson{{nb...|in Twelve Volumes}}|edition=federal|location=New York, N.Y.&#59; London|publisher=[[w:G. P. Putnam's Sons|G[eorge] P[almer] Putnam’s Sons]]{{nb...|The Knickerbocker Press}}|year_published=1905|volume=XII|pages=59–60|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=i3jCAwAAQBAJ&pg=PA60|oclc=682118076|passage=Amid these enlarged measures, the papers tell us of one by the legislature of New York, so much in the opposite direction that it would puzzle us to say in what, the darkest age of the history of bigotry and barbarism, we should find an apt place for it. ''It is said'' they have declared by law that all those who hereafter shall join in communion with the religious sect of Shaking quakers, shall be deemed civilly dead, their marriage vows dissolved, and all their children and property taken from them; without any provision for rehabilitation in case of '''resipiscence'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Hallam England|volume=I|chapter=From the Dissolution of [[w:Charles I of England|Charles {{quote-gloss|I}}]]’s Third Parliament to the Meeting of the Long Parliament|page=525|passage=The church of England was no longer exasperated against them; if there was ever any prosecution, it was to screen the king from the reproach of the puritans. They drew a flattering picture of the '''resipiscence''' of the Anglican party; who are come to acknowledge the truth in some articles, and differ in others rather verbally than in substance, or in points not fundamental; {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1863|author=Ernest Naville|chapter=Seventh Lecture. Religion.|title=Life Eternal.{{nb...|From the French of M. Ernest Naville, Late Professor of Philosophy in the University of Geneva. Translated by Special Permission.}}|location=London|publisher=W. H. Dalton,{{nb...|bookseller to the Queen, 28, Cockspur Street.}}|page=221|pageurl=https://books.google.com.sg/books?id=oyO4Yp5R84oC&pg=PA221|oclc=7962009|passage=[G]reat intellect knows no '''resipiscence''' and high art has nothing to repent of.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1887|author=w:Henry Charles Lea|chapter=Bohemia|title=A History of the Inquisition of the Middle Ages.{{nb...|[...] In Three Volumes.}}|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Harper (publisher)|Harper & Brothers]],{{nb...|Franklin Square}}|volume=II|page=491|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/historyofinquisiw02leah/page/491/mode/1up|oclc=1315744|passage=Seven bishops arrayed him {{quote-gloss|{{w|Jan Hus}}}} in priestly garb and warned him to recant while yet there was time. He turned to the crowd, and with broken voice declared that he could not confess the errors which he had never entertained, let he should lie to God, when the bishops interrupted him, crying that they had waited long enough, for he was obstinate in his heresy. {{...}} There had already been afforded ample opportunity for '''resipiscence''', and the convict could always still recant up to the lighting of the fagots.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1958|quotee=w:John of Damascus|chapter=Introduction|translator=Frederic H[athaway] Chase, Jr.|title=Saint John of Damascus: Writings|series=The Fathers of the Church: A New Translation|seriesvolume=37|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=Fathers of the Church|page=xiii|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=GIEwAAAAYAAJ&pg=PR13|oclc=13279194|passage=[I]f a king preach a gospel to you other than that which you have received, close your ears. For I still hesitate to say, as did the Apostle ([[w:Epistle to the Galatians|Gal{{quote-gloss|atians}}]] 1:8), ''let him be anathema'', as long as I see any possibility of '''resipiscence'''&nbsp;… it does not belong to kings to legislate for the Church&nbsp;… to kings belongs the maintenance of civil order, but the administration of the Church belongs to the shepherds and teachers.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2005|author=w:Gilles Quispel|chapter={{w|Plotinus}} and the Jewish Gnōstikoi|editor=w:Johannes van Oort|title=Gnostica, Judaica, Catholica: Collected Essays of Gilles Quispel|series=Nag Hammadi and Manichaean Studies|seriesvolume=55|location=Leiden, South Holland|publisher=[[w:Brill Publishers|Brill]]|year_published=2008|section=part II (Judaica)|page=590|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=bqRGI6bugRcC&pg=PA590|isbn=978-90-04-13945-9|issn=0929-2470|passage=What to say of the other notions (''hypostasis'') they introduce, exiles (''paroikia''), reflections (''antitypos'') and '''resipiscences''' (''metanoia''). If they say that that are passions of the Soul (''Psyche'', Sophia) when she comes to herself ''and reflections'', when she beholds symbols of Reality but not Reality itself, that is characteristic of people who use resounding neologisms to make propaganda for their sect.|footer={{small|A translation from Plotinus’s ''Against the Gnostics'' II, 9, 6, 1–6.}}}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} The [[act#Noun|act]] of [[become|becoming]] [[comprehending#Adjective|comprehending]], [[reasonable]] or [[responsible#Adjective|responsible]], especially after having [[behave]]d in an [[uncomprehending]], [[unreasonable]], or [[irresponsible#Adjective|irresponsible]] [[manner#Noun|manner]]; the act of [[come to one's senses|coming to one's senses]]; {{lb|en|countable}} an instance of this.
#* {{quote-book|en|date=17 December 1928|author=[[w:J. Reuben Clark|J[oshua] Reuben Clark]]|quotee=w:Leopold II, Holy Roman Emperor|chapter=The Grand Alliance and the Holy Alliance [Leopold II to the Empress of Russia, the Electress of Mayence, and the Kings of England, Prussia, Spain, Sicily, and Sardinia]|title=Memorandum on the Monroe Doctrine|location=Washington, D.C.|publisher=[[w:United States Government Publishing Office|United States Government Printing Office]]|year_published=1930|pages=45–46|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=z8qOAAAAMAAJ&pg=PA46|oclc=1203427641|passage=The most urgent thing to do seems to be that we come all together in having at once our Ministers in France give a joint declaration or like and simultaneous declarations which may bring to their senses the leaders of the violent party {{quote-gloss|behind the French Revolution}} and avert desperate decisions by still leaving open to them a way for honest '''resipiscence''' and the peaceful establishment of a condition of things in France that will at least save the dignity of the Crown and the essential consideration of general tranquillity, {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1973|author=w:Oliver Sacks|chapter=&#91;Perspectives&#93; Awakening|chapterurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=iGvdKOjgq0UC&pg=PT272|title=[[w:Awakenings (book)|Awakenings]]|location=London|publisher=[[w:Picador (imprint)|Picador]]|year_published=2011|isbn=978-0-330-53717-9|passage=This return-to-oneself, '''resipiscence''', 'rebirth,' is an infinitely dramatic and moving event, especially in a patient with a rich and full self, who has been dispossessed by disease for years or decades {{...}} That a return to health or '''resipiscence''' is possible, in these patients with half a century of the profoundest illness, must fill one with a sense of amazement—that the potential for health and self can survive, after so much of the life and structure of the person has been lost, and after so long and exclusive an immersion in sickness.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1996|author=w:Jean-Bertrand Aristide|chapter=Hope|translator=Carrol F. Coates|title=Dignity|location=Charlottesville, Va.&#59; London|publisher=[[w:University of Virginia Press|University Press of Virginia]]|page=123|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=uZcXYvBhyNIC&pg=PA123|isbn=978-0-8139-1674-3|passage=On June 16, 1993, the UN voted the embargo on petroleum and arms. With Bazin gone, there was not even a puppet government. After eighty-nine weeks (and how many thousands of hours of verbiage), the international community was finally headed in the only direction that might bring the military to '''resipiscence'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2012|author=w:Will Self|chapter=|title=[[w:Umbrella (novel)|Umbrella]]|location=London|publisher=w:Bloomsbury Publishing|year_published=2013|page=211|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=n8t1TMGWcRMC&pg=PA211|isbn=978-1-4088-3167-0|passage=All this is, Busner thinks, still inadequate to the task of expressing the quality of her '''resipiscence'''&nbsp;– a return to good health of a miraculous nature.}}

